Technically this is also true, though there are rationalizations that
support "simply remove the object from the bones" as a better solution 
for this case:

-- Rodney never permanently dies, so he retrieves the Book of the Dead;

-- Moloch's high priest is an _office_, not a specific being; the new 
high priest retrieves the Amulet and sets up shop in a new Sanctum;

-- Vlad gets restored to unlife (Dracula always comes back, remember) and 
comes and fetches the Candelabrum;

-- Your Quest Nemesis, who is clearly not dead in the new character's 
universe, comes and gets the Bell of Opening.

These rationalizations get a little bit weaker when you start discussing
unique artifacts like Orcrist and Sting, but fortunately those items
aren't guaranteed to always exist, so there's no need to prevent bones
from being saved or remove the object from the bones, instead stopping
the bones from being loaded.  It's the "convert to a normal object" bit 
that is an odd, yet functional workaround.

